WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.745 --> 00:00:02.864
- I think the secret to my career is,

00:00:02.864 --> 00:00:04.663
I don't know what I can't do.

00:00:04.663 --> 00:00:05.496
These things happen to you,

00:00:05.496 --> 00:00:07.010
and you think you've been
dealt a terrible hand,

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:07.843
or you've had bad luck,

00:00:07.843 --> 00:00:09.522
and when you just go with it,

00:00:09.522 --> 00:00:11.736
you just start improvising,

00:00:11.736 --> 00:00:14.486
suddenly you realize that you stumble upon

00:00:14.486 --> 00:00:16.602
some of the best things that
have ever happened to you.

00:00:16.602 --> 00:00:18.009
Or success is a little
like a white tuxedo,

00:00:18.009 --> 00:00:21.000
it looks good, but you're very
afraid of getting it dirty.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.530
And it can inhibit you.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.118
- He's an American TV
show host, comedy writer,

00:00:25.118 --> 00:00:26.597
and television producer.

00:00:26.597 --> 00:00:29.401
He's best known for hosting
several late night talk shows.

00:00:29.401 --> 00:00:33.211
Since 2010, he's hosted Conan
on the cable channel TBS.

00:00:33.211 --> 00:00:34.536
He's Conan O'Brien,

00:00:34.536 --> 00:00:37.163
and here's my take on his
top 10 rules to success.

00:00:37.163 --> 00:00:38.994
Rule number two is my personal favorite.

00:00:38.994 --> 00:00:40.835
And make sure to stick around
all the way until the end

00:00:40.835 --> 00:00:42.864
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:42.864 --> 00:00:43.697
And as always,

00:00:43.697 --> 00:00:45.571
if he says something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:45.571 --> 00:00:47.291
make sure to leave it
in the comments below

00:00:47.291 --> 00:00:48.478
and put quotes around it,

00:00:48.478 --> 00:00:51.811
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.072
- I think the secret to my career is,

00:01:06.072 --> 00:01:08.489
I don't know what I can't do.

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:12.836
There are times when I should
probably sit and think,

00:01:12.836 --> 00:01:16.463
do I really want to jump out
there with Phillip Seymore,

00:01:16.463 --> 00:01:19.007
I mean with John C. Reilly,

00:01:19.007 --> 00:01:22.345
and sing The Night Chicago Died?

00:01:22.345 --> 00:01:26.134
Or do I really want to play
the blues with Lil' Ed?

00:01:26.134 --> 00:01:27.179
And you just do it.

00:01:27.179 --> 00:01:29.310
You just do these things.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.482
- [Charlie] And the audience
wants you to do these things?

00:01:32.482 --> 00:01:34.676
- Yeah, I think what's,

00:01:34.676 --> 00:01:36.139
I don't know what it is,

00:01:36.139 --> 00:01:37.328
but I think over the years

00:01:37.328 --> 00:01:40.029
people have gotten
comfortable with the notion

00:01:40.029 --> 00:01:42.057
that I try things.

00:01:42.057 --> 00:01:43.508
- [Charlie] It's almost put to (mumbles).

00:01:43.508 --> 00:01:45.773
- And they're not worried about me.

00:01:45.773 --> 00:01:46.606
Do you know what I mean?

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:48.215
I think that's the key.

00:01:48.215 --> 00:01:51.882
People aren't worried
about me getting hurt.

00:01:55.712 --> 00:01:57.407
I'll give it 100%,

00:01:57.407 --> 00:01:59.384
and then if it completely
falls on its face,

00:01:59.384 --> 00:02:01.167
I'll laugh about it and move on.

00:02:01.167 --> 00:02:03.250
And I think there's a
little bit of a sense,

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:06.059
after all these years, people say,

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:07.421
"All right, he tried it.

00:02:07.421 --> 00:02:08.805
"Maybe it worked, maybe it didn't work,

00:02:08.805 --> 00:02:11.102
"but he had a good time."

00:02:11.102 --> 00:02:13.236
- And for you, you can
come back the next night,

00:02:13.236 --> 00:02:16.108
and the next night, and the next night?

00:02:16.108 --> 00:02:17.941
- Yeah, I mean that's,

00:02:20.478 --> 00:02:21.561
I don't know.

00:02:23.298 --> 00:02:24.807
Over the years I've realized

00:02:24.807 --> 00:02:27.520
that there are a lot of comedians

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.687
that are very worried about
keeping their dignity intact,

00:02:33.463 --> 00:02:35.566
and keeping their personal space,

00:02:35.566 --> 00:02:38.047
and keep their sense of authority.

00:02:38.047 --> 00:02:39.951
And for better or worse,
and it might be worse,

00:02:39.951 --> 00:02:41.793
I don't have that.

00:02:41.793 --> 00:02:44.496
Do you know what I mean?

00:02:44.496 --> 00:02:46.413
The people that I've
always liked growing up,

00:02:46.413 --> 00:02:47.600
and I'm not just talking about late night,

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.305
I'm talking about anybody
in movie, in film.

00:02:51.305 --> 00:02:53.683
The people I liked, Peter Sellers, they--

00:02:53.683 --> 00:02:54.902
- [Charlie] Would let it all hang out?

00:02:54.902 --> 00:02:56.738
- They just go.

00:02:56.738 --> 00:02:59.237
If you look at a lot of those
old classic Carson clips,

00:02:59.237 --> 00:03:02.328
he's jumping into hot
tubs with Don Rickles,

00:03:02.328 --> 00:03:04.522
he's jumping through fake walls,

00:03:04.522 --> 00:03:06.737
he's falling on his ass,

00:03:06.737 --> 00:03:09.070
he's dressed as Floyd Turbo.

00:03:11.461 --> 00:03:12.979
So sometimes I look back to that.

00:03:12.979 --> 00:03:14.002
And I remembered,

00:03:14.002 --> 00:03:16.776
"Well, Johnny just went for it."

00:03:16.776 --> 00:03:20.194
So for better or worse, I'll try.

00:03:20.194 --> 00:03:21.704
I'll get out there.

00:03:21.704 --> 00:03:23.360
I'm very physical.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:24.699
My wife is always horrified.

00:03:24.699 --> 00:03:25.919
I'm always coming out of the shower

00:03:25.919 --> 00:03:27.946
just covered in bruises.

00:03:27.946 --> 00:03:29.212
And she'll say, "What was that?"

00:03:29.212 --> 00:03:30.567
And I'll say, "Oh, I did a bit.

00:03:30.567 --> 00:03:32.884
"We had Jeff Garlin on the show,

00:03:32.884 --> 00:03:36.154
"and he and I rolled
down a flight of steps.

00:03:36.154 --> 00:03:38.627
"And I hit a fire hydrant."

00:03:38.627 --> 00:03:40.467
What astounds me is how many
young people come up to me

00:03:40.467 --> 00:03:42.497
and say, "Conan, someday I'm
going to be on your show."

00:03:42.497 --> 00:03:43.330
- [Charlie] Me too.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:45.894
- And I say, "What is it you do?"

00:03:45.894 --> 00:03:47.008
They don't know.

00:03:47.008 --> 00:03:47.841
That's the crazy thing.

00:03:47.841 --> 00:03:50.319
Most people, it's the
Paris Hilton phenomenon,

00:03:50.319 --> 00:03:51.825
they just want to be famous.

00:03:51.825 --> 00:03:53.601
And they've actually seen it work.

00:03:53.601 --> 00:03:56.100
They've seen someone get incredibly famous

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.933
for just being famous.

00:03:59.912 --> 00:04:01.970
So that's a little unsettling sometimes.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.470
But mostly, what I've noticed,

00:04:06.002 --> 00:04:08.335
or what I would say to people is,

00:04:08.335 --> 00:04:11.900
"You've got to go to where
they're making the thing

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:15.731
"that you like, and then
work there in any capacity."

00:04:15.731 --> 00:04:18.319
"If you have to get coffee for somebody,

00:04:18.319 --> 00:04:19.904
"if you have to hold a cable,

00:04:19.904 --> 00:04:23.101
"if you have to stand there and volunteer,

00:04:23.101 --> 00:04:25.864
"go to where they're
making the thing that you,

00:04:25.864 --> 00:04:28.376
"that moves you, that gets you excited,

00:04:28.376 --> 00:04:30.876
"and try and get close to it."

00:04:32.247 --> 00:04:34.652
I mean, I remembered for years I was aware

00:04:34.652 --> 00:04:38.103
that I'm not doing the
thing that is going to be

00:04:38.103 --> 00:04:40.822
my life's work, but I'm close.

00:04:40.822 --> 00:04:43.368
I couldn't even tell
you exactly what it was,

00:04:43.368 --> 00:04:45.135
but I knew I was close.

00:04:45.135 --> 00:04:46.203
Do you know what I mean?

00:04:46.203 --> 00:04:48.016
I knew when I was a writer
at Saturday Night Live,

00:04:48.016 --> 00:04:49.951
"This isn't quite it, but I'm close."

00:04:49.951 --> 00:04:52.142
I knew when I was a
writer in the Simpsons,

00:04:52.142 --> 00:04:54.018
"This is further way from it

00:04:54.018 --> 00:04:55.953
than I was at Saturday Night Live."

00:04:55.953 --> 00:05:00.758
I knew that I liked being
around the live performing.

00:05:00.758 --> 00:05:03.690
So I knew when I was close.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:04.523
And that's what I tell them,

00:05:04.523 --> 00:05:06.115
is just go there and don't
have any ego about it.

00:05:06.115 --> 00:05:07.943
There's so many channels
now, so many stations.

00:05:07.943 --> 00:05:09.376
- [Charlie] That's
exactly what I tell them.

00:05:09.376 --> 00:05:11.157
And just get in the arena is the idea.

00:05:11.157 --> 00:05:12.346
I mean, if you get it,

00:05:12.346 --> 00:05:14.478
80% or 90% of it is getting in the arena.

00:05:14.478 --> 00:05:16.561
- Get in there and do it.

00:05:17.439 --> 00:05:21.026
And eventually, the Brownian
movement of molecules,

00:05:21.026 --> 00:05:23.339
eventually you getting in there

00:05:23.339 --> 00:05:25.644
and putting yourself out there.

00:05:25.644 --> 00:05:27.075
If you have something to offer,

00:05:27.075 --> 00:05:29.227
someone's going to see it at some point,

00:05:29.227 --> 00:05:30.391
and you're off and running.

00:05:30.391 --> 00:05:31.806
So many people come up to me and say,

00:05:31.806 --> 00:05:33.103
"Now that you've made it,

00:05:33.103 --> 00:05:35.084
"don't you just want to stick
it to some of those people?"

00:05:35.084 --> 00:05:36.242
And I think no.

00:05:36.242 --> 00:05:38.697
Because they weren't wrong,

00:05:38.697 --> 00:05:41.780
we had our problems in the beginning.

00:05:44.306 --> 00:05:45.383
This had to be a long process,

00:05:45.383 --> 00:05:47.227
that's just the way it had to be.

00:05:47.227 --> 00:05:48.643
And I prefer it this way.

00:05:48.643 --> 00:05:49.849
I was raised Irish Catholic.

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:53.093
I had a sense of wanting to earn it.

00:05:53.093 --> 00:05:55.195
And there was nothing
that was more difficult

00:05:55.195 --> 00:05:58.862
than being proclaimed
Letterman's successor,

00:06:00.194 --> 00:06:02.260
and having everybody have
this uneasy sense of,

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:03.177
"Who is he?

00:06:03.177 --> 00:06:05.158
"Should he have this?"
- "And what has he ever done?"

00:06:05.158 --> 00:06:06.512
- "And what has he ever done?

00:06:06.512 --> 00:06:08.415
"And why does he get to do this?"

00:06:08.415 --> 00:06:12.242
And maybe this whole
process was just necessary.

00:06:12.242 --> 00:06:14.742
This was my way of earning it.

00:06:15.818 --> 00:06:19.112
And I think a lot of the
confidence I have on the air now

00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:23.457
is because I went through
a very difficult period,

00:06:23.457 --> 00:06:25.482
and there's nobody out there who can say,

00:06:25.482 --> 00:06:27.371
"Gee, he had it easy."

00:06:27.371 --> 00:06:30.788
I mean, I'm famous for having to overcome

00:06:32.236 --> 00:06:34.830
a lot of obstacles in
front of people on the air.

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:38.971
And so I'm comfortable with
the way it all worked out.

00:06:38.971 --> 00:06:41.748
- When did you decide you
could professionally silly?

00:06:41.748 --> 00:06:43.929
Did you get a glimmer of that at Harvard,

00:06:43.929 --> 00:06:45.706
that you were taking Cairo?
- Yeah, well the Lampoon

00:06:45.706 --> 00:06:48.358
was a big influence because I was,

00:06:48.358 --> 00:06:50.673
you don't learn to be funny,

00:06:50.673 --> 00:06:52.838
you don't learn to be silly.

00:06:52.838 --> 00:06:56.736
A lot of what you are or who you are,

00:06:56.736 --> 00:07:00.362
in some respects is decided,
I think, early in life.

00:07:00.362 --> 00:07:02.556
You get the basic ingredients.

00:07:02.556 --> 00:07:04.476
To me, when I was a kid,

00:07:04.476 --> 00:07:06.775
and I think we all do this
in one way or another,

00:07:06.775 --> 00:07:10.108
I ran through the list, "What do I have?

00:07:11.344 --> 00:07:12.949
"What do I have?

00:07:12.949 --> 00:07:16.558
"What are my skills that will help me?"

00:07:16.558 --> 00:07:19.391
A, not get picked on or beaten up.

00:07:20.351 --> 00:07:23.049
And that's in my own family.

00:07:23.049 --> 00:07:27.384
And B, that maybe get the
attention of that girl I like.

00:07:27.384 --> 00:07:29.246
Those are the things
that are operating on you

00:07:29.246 --> 00:07:31.244
in this very elemental level.

00:07:31.244 --> 00:07:33.182
Those are the things that
you're thinking about

00:07:33.182 --> 00:07:35.954
when you're young, and you're a kid.

00:07:35.954 --> 00:07:37.083
"Am I good athlete?"

00:07:37.083 --> 00:07:39.521
No, I was a terrible athlete.

00:07:39.521 --> 00:07:42.371
"Was I incredibly good looking,
and all the girls liked me?"

00:07:42.371 --> 00:07:43.796
No.

00:07:43.796 --> 00:07:45.794
I had this laundry list of
things I would go through,

00:07:45.794 --> 00:07:47.196
and then I had this one thing,

00:07:47.196 --> 00:07:48.490
which was I could make people laugh.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:49.439
And so what happens, I think,

00:07:49.439 --> 00:07:51.634
you find the thing you're good at,

00:07:51.634 --> 00:07:53.584
and in my case it was so glaring.

00:07:53.584 --> 00:07:55.243
It's not like there were
a million other things.

00:07:55.243 --> 00:07:57.881
There was this one thing.

00:07:57.881 --> 00:08:00.820
And if I had been a pretty
good baseball player,

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:02.903
I wouldn't be here today.

00:08:04.569 --> 00:08:06.819
So I kept hyper-developing.

00:08:07.942 --> 00:08:12.109
You could say I was hyper-developing
a defense mechanism.

00:08:13.243 --> 00:08:14.838
And if you look at a lot
of artists out there,

00:08:14.838 --> 00:08:16.789
that's probably what a
lot of them are doing.

00:08:16.789 --> 00:08:18.983
My father, who is here,
and is a brilliant guy,

00:08:18.983 --> 00:08:20.931
once said to me, "I get it now.

00:08:20.931 --> 00:08:22.566
"You're making your
living off of something

00:08:22.566 --> 00:08:25.078
"that should probably be treated."

00:08:25.078 --> 00:08:27.531
(Conan and audience laughs)

00:08:27.531 --> 00:08:28.781
And he's right.

00:08:31.069 --> 00:08:32.402
And then a tear.

00:08:34.025 --> 00:08:35.608
But a wealthy tear.

00:08:38.749 --> 00:08:39.680
Not important.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:41.699
Don't pursue wealth.

00:08:41.699 --> 00:08:44.181
(audience laughs)

00:08:44.181 --> 00:08:45.931
But that is something

00:08:48.356 --> 00:08:53.109
that I think many of us do,
in one respect or another,

00:08:53.109 --> 00:08:56.147
is we double down on
the thing that we have,

00:08:56.147 --> 00:08:58.150
that we have to offer.

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:00.425
- You don't consider yourself
in the late night war,

00:09:00.425 --> 00:09:01.274
so to speak,

00:09:01.274 --> 00:09:03.850
because you're on at 12:30
rather than 11:00 or 11:30?

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:05.517
- I don't feel that,

00:09:06.519 --> 00:09:10.724
sometimes I'll bump into
someone on the street,

00:09:10.724 --> 00:09:14.778
and they'll say, "Hey, good
luck in your war against Dave."

00:09:14.778 --> 00:09:16.314
And I'll think, "I have no problem."

00:09:16.314 --> 00:09:18.598
I'm on after Dave.

00:09:18.598 --> 00:09:20.152
He's doing a good show.

00:09:20.152 --> 00:09:22.468
And I wish him well.

00:09:22.468 --> 00:09:25.468
And I'm not in competition with him.

00:09:26.867 --> 00:09:29.542
Sometimes I'll see
cartoons in the newspaper

00:09:29.542 --> 00:09:30.433
of I'm wearing boxing gloves,

00:09:30.433 --> 00:09:32.775
and Dave's wearing boxing gloves,

00:09:32.775 --> 00:09:33.972
and I think, "I'm not--"

00:09:33.972 --> 00:09:35.244
- [Charlie] You're not in the same place."

00:09:35.244 --> 00:09:37.106
- "I'm not boxing anybody."

00:09:37.106 --> 00:09:39.374
But no, I don't really feel like I'm,

00:09:39.374 --> 00:09:42.201
I don't feel like I'm in
competition with anyone else.

00:09:42.201 --> 00:09:44.804
I most of all feel like I'm
in competition with myself

00:09:44.804 --> 00:09:46.894
to do the best show I can do.

00:09:46.894 --> 00:09:50.577
The last three and a half months
has been all improvisation.

00:09:50.577 --> 00:09:53.850
The groundswell of internet support

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:56.847
from a lot of young people
that are in this room

00:09:56.847 --> 00:09:59.811
completely took my network by surprise,

00:09:59.811 --> 00:10:01.897
they don't know what hit them.

00:10:01.897 --> 00:10:04.213
I think there's a lot of
people in broadcast television

00:10:04.213 --> 00:10:06.296
that are very dismissive,

00:10:07.966 --> 00:10:10.735
or have been very dismissive
about the internet,

00:10:10.735 --> 00:10:12.790
and they're all so afraid of it.

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:16.752
And they tend to deride
what they don't understand.

00:10:16.752 --> 00:10:19.921
So when this explosion
happened on the internet,

00:10:19.921 --> 00:10:21.513
when they announced that,

00:10:21.513 --> 00:10:24.099
"Well, okay, maybe we're
going to slide Conan

00:10:24.099 --> 00:10:26.569
"over to accommodate this other gentleman

00:10:26.569 --> 00:10:29.190
"who's having difficulties
in another time period."

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:32.055
And I won't get into specifics,
you'll have to look it up.

00:10:32.055 --> 00:10:33.839
(audience laughs)

00:10:33.839 --> 00:10:35.807
And I said, "You know what?

00:10:35.807 --> 00:10:37.389
"That doesn't really work for me."

00:10:37.389 --> 00:10:40.501
I think in a fairly polite way.

00:10:40.501 --> 00:10:45.074
There was suddenly a huge
reaction from people,

00:10:45.074 --> 00:10:46.442
some of the people in this room,

00:10:46.442 --> 00:10:48.421
a lot of people like you
across the country said,

00:10:48.421 --> 00:10:51.027
"Wait a minute, we like this person.

00:10:51.027 --> 00:10:53.360
"And this person kind of is,

00:10:55.109 --> 00:10:56.249
"we're with him."

00:10:56.249 --> 00:10:58.180
And they started reacting on the internet.

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:02.022
And the first thing that
happened at my employers'.

00:11:02.022 --> 00:11:03.959
They saw this huge
explosion on the internet,

00:11:03.959 --> 00:11:06.645
and they thought that I was doing it.

00:11:06.645 --> 00:11:09.906
And they really had this
attitude of, "Make him stop.

00:11:09.906 --> 00:11:11.385
"Why is he doing this?"

00:11:11.385 --> 00:11:13.685
And they just didn't
understand what was happening.

00:11:13.685 --> 00:11:16.027
I think they still don't
understand what's happening.

00:11:16.027 --> 00:11:17.634
And my feeling is,

00:11:17.634 --> 00:11:20.829
what I've learned is I
had nowhere else to go,

00:11:20.829 --> 00:11:23.071
so I started on Twitter,

00:11:23.071 --> 00:11:26.623
because I literally had no other option.

00:11:26.623 --> 00:11:30.053
I was and am legally
prohibited from appearing on

00:11:30.053 --> 00:11:34.107
television, radio, and doing
performances on the internet.

00:11:34.107 --> 00:11:36.775
So I was just literally like a prisoner

00:11:36.775 --> 00:11:38.847
in a 14th century cell,

00:11:38.847 --> 00:11:40.811
writing little things on a scrap of paper

00:11:40.811 --> 00:11:43.968
and throwing them out the window.

00:11:43.968 --> 00:11:48.163
And hoping a peasant would
by, "Eh, what's this?

00:11:48.163 --> 00:11:49.830
"He's in the tower."

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:52.660
So I started to do that,

00:11:52.660 --> 00:11:54.110
and send out these little things.

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:56.277
And it exploded overnight.

00:11:57.262 --> 00:11:59.589
And at first I started to
hear a little bit of stuff

00:11:59.589 --> 00:12:00.961
from the other side saying,

00:12:00.961 --> 00:12:03.678
"We're not sure you should be allowed to,

00:12:03.678 --> 00:12:04.774
"because of the,"

00:12:04.774 --> 00:12:07.030
and then they realized the absurdity

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:10.030
of shutting down my Twitter account.

00:12:12.576 --> 00:12:14.186
So that started with that.

00:12:14.186 --> 00:12:15.997
And then I started to
think about the tour,

00:12:15.997 --> 00:12:17.659
which I'm allowed to do.

00:12:17.659 --> 00:12:20.362
And so we started this idea for a tour.

00:12:20.362 --> 00:12:22.718
And then what was fascinating is,

00:12:22.718 --> 00:12:25.217
by the time we launched the
tour, or announced the tour,

00:12:25.217 --> 00:12:26.697
I did not do one,

00:12:26.697 --> 00:12:29.531
I didn't spend on penny on advertising.

00:12:29.531 --> 00:12:33.698
I sent out one tweet that
directed people to a website

00:12:35.428 --> 00:12:36.954
where you could buy your ticket.

00:12:36.954 --> 00:12:38.219
That was it.

00:12:38.219 --> 00:12:40.355
And the show sold out in a couple of hours

00:12:40.355 --> 00:12:41.491
across the country.

00:12:41.491 --> 00:12:43.414
And that's got everybody, a lot of people,

00:12:43.414 --> 00:12:47.164
rethinking how things are marketed.

00:12:47.164 --> 00:12:49.393
And there's not one billboard.

00:12:49.393 --> 00:12:51.158
I didn't have to go one radio station,

00:12:51.158 --> 00:12:54.055
and sit with morning DJs,

00:12:54.055 --> 00:12:55.383
and hawk my show.

00:12:55.383 --> 00:12:56.506
I didn't have to do any of that.

00:12:56.506 --> 00:12:57.969
It was one tweet.

00:12:57.969 --> 00:12:59.780
And I think people were
starting to understand

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:03.110
that the world has completely changed.

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:05.277
And it has, it is staying.

00:13:06.588 --> 00:13:09.171
"And I think we can do better."

00:13:10.032 --> 00:13:10.865
Sorry.

00:13:11.799 --> 00:13:12.714
- That was great.

00:13:12.714 --> 00:13:16.214
- I forgot I wasn't running for something.

00:13:17.565 --> 00:13:19.857
But I think that's what's,

00:13:19.857 --> 00:13:21.258
the biggest lesson that I've learned

00:13:21.258 --> 00:13:22.718
in the last three and a half months is,

00:13:22.718 --> 00:13:24.164
it's just a good life lesson.

00:13:24.164 --> 00:13:25.613
And I'm not trying to
sound corny or anything,

00:13:25.613 --> 00:13:27.178
but these happen to you,

00:13:27.178 --> 00:13:28.826
and you think you've been
dealt a terrible hand

00:13:28.826 --> 00:13:29.910
or you've had a bad luck.

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:31.195
And when you just go with it,

00:13:31.195 --> 00:13:33.580
you just start improvising,

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:36.262
suddenly you realize that you stumble upon

00:13:36.262 --> 00:13:39.475
some of the best things that
have ever happened to you.

00:13:39.475 --> 00:13:40.955
And what's interesting about Twitter,

00:13:40.955 --> 00:13:42.492
is that because you're limited to,

00:13:42.492 --> 00:13:44.626
I think it's 140 characters.

00:13:44.626 --> 00:13:46.145
Someone's going to correct me right now.

00:13:46.145 --> 00:13:47.545
"Not so."

00:13:47.545 --> 00:13:50.378
(audience laughs)

00:14:00.724 --> 00:14:03.051
You're all going to rush the stage.

00:14:03.051 --> 00:14:05.276
Beat the crap out of me.

00:14:05.276 --> 00:14:06.332
But because you're limited,

00:14:06.332 --> 00:14:08.248
it's actually a great comedy writing tool.

00:14:08.248 --> 00:14:10.172
You're forced, there's
this economy of words,

00:14:10.172 --> 00:14:11.568
so I'm constantly writing things,

00:14:11.568 --> 00:14:14.019
and then I run them past Bleyaert,

00:14:14.019 --> 00:14:16.911
Big Bley, who's taking
the pictures over there,

00:14:16.911 --> 00:14:20.411
as if he doesn't have enough photos of me.

00:14:23.302 --> 00:14:26.531
And he'll say, "Well, that's
actually three words over."

00:14:26.531 --> 00:14:28.929
And it forces you to look
back at the sentence,

00:14:28.929 --> 00:14:32.326
and it forces you to
crystallize your comedy idea,

00:14:32.326 --> 00:14:33.879
which is fascinating.

00:14:33.879 --> 00:14:35.523
And the other thing is I've been,

00:14:35.523 --> 00:14:36.914
this whole tour wouldn't have happened.

00:14:36.914 --> 00:14:38.408
This tour is a dream come true from me.

00:14:38.408 --> 00:14:39.871
I've always wanted to,

00:14:39.871 --> 00:14:43.038
it's half rock show, half comedy show.

00:14:43.876 --> 00:14:48.326
And then it's this
fantasy to get to do this.

00:14:48.326 --> 00:14:49.741
So the last three and a half months

00:14:49.741 --> 00:14:51.965
have been the most interesting
time in my entire career,

00:14:51.965 --> 00:14:54.512
and I wouldn't have traded
this for anything in the world.

00:14:54.512 --> 00:14:56.798
And so three and a half months ago,

00:14:56.798 --> 00:14:58.533
it looked to everybody like bad luck

00:14:58.533 --> 00:15:01.094
has become amazingly good luck.

00:15:01.094 --> 00:15:04.427
And I think that is a lot of what we're,

00:15:05.574 --> 00:15:07.920
what relates to everybody here.

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:10.141
A lot of you are in your twenties,

00:15:10.141 --> 00:15:11.821
and you take for granted that,

00:15:11.821 --> 00:15:14.076
well, this is the way
the world is right now.

00:15:14.076 --> 00:15:17.703
But from my perspective,
it's changed dramatically

00:15:17.703 --> 00:15:19.430
in just seven years.

00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:21.018
And I don't even know
where we're going to be

00:15:21.018 --> 00:15:22.723
in five years from now.

00:15:22.723 --> 00:15:24.274
So I don't know what
television's going to be

00:15:24.274 --> 00:15:25.107
five years from now.

00:15:25.107 --> 00:15:26.267
There's a lot of people that think

00:15:26.267 --> 00:15:29.345
you're just going to experience
it all through your server.

00:15:29.345 --> 00:15:32.168
And people don't even know how
the business is going to change.

00:15:32.168 --> 00:15:33.296
There might not be, really,

00:15:33.296 --> 00:15:35.272
network television as we know it.

00:15:35.272 --> 00:15:37.093
Wouldn't that be sweet?

00:15:37.093 --> 00:15:38.282
(audience laughs)

00:15:38.282 --> 00:15:39.946
So you know what I mean?

00:15:39.946 --> 00:15:40.779
Who knows?

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:44.857
Seize the day, carpe diem.

00:15:46.186 --> 00:15:49.383
I hate it when people say
carpe diem, I really do.

00:15:49.383 --> 00:15:51.638
I just wanted to work it in.

00:15:51.638 --> 00:15:52.756
I'm going to keep talking to you.

00:15:52.756 --> 00:15:54.065
Ask me another question.

00:15:54.065 --> 00:15:56.349
Expect difficulties is something

00:15:56.349 --> 00:16:00.348
I think everybody in this
room has gotten into Harvard.

00:16:00.348 --> 00:16:02.669
Some of you who probably
are here snuck in,

00:16:02.669 --> 00:16:04.175
but I don't care.

00:16:04.175 --> 00:16:05.503
Let's just say for point of argument

00:16:05.503 --> 00:16:08.002
that everybody here got into Harvard,

00:16:08.002 --> 00:16:11.476
and you feel this incredible
pressure to succeed.

00:16:11.476 --> 00:16:13.823
And I gave a speech here back in 2000,

00:16:13.823 --> 00:16:15.123
and at the end I said,

00:16:15.123 --> 00:16:17.793
"Fame is a little like a
white tuxedo, it looks good."

00:16:17.793 --> 00:16:19.344
Or, "Success is a little
like a white tuxedo.

00:16:19.344 --> 00:16:22.257
"It looks good but you're very
afraid of getting it dirty.

00:16:22.257 --> 00:16:24.159
"And it can inhibit you."

00:16:24.159 --> 00:16:27.826
And I think I would
tell my 19 year old self

00:16:29.632 --> 00:16:32.497
that everything you're feeling right now,

00:16:32.497 --> 00:16:36.365
I used to think that if I could
look at someone like myself,

00:16:36.365 --> 00:16:39.950
I thought they had figured it
all out, and they were fine.

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:43.912
And wouldn't it be magical
if I could have my own show,

00:16:43.912 --> 00:16:46.470
and be funny, and everyone knew who I was,

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:47.779
and most people liked it?

00:16:47.779 --> 00:16:49.002
That will be a great thing,

00:16:49.002 --> 00:16:50.567
and it would solve all my problems.

00:16:50.567 --> 00:16:54.734
And I would like my future self
to say that's not the case,

00:16:56.192 --> 00:16:57.943
it's actually day by day,

00:16:57.943 --> 00:16:59.088
how you do your work,

00:16:59.088 --> 00:17:01.218
how you conduct yourself,

00:17:01.218 --> 00:17:02.968
how you treat people,

00:17:03.885 --> 00:17:06.137
the joy you get out of your work.

00:17:06.137 --> 00:17:08.027
That that is far more important.

00:17:08.027 --> 00:17:10.694
And I'm not saying that as like,

00:17:12.445 --> 00:17:14.244
to try and take away from,

00:17:14.244 --> 00:17:17.718
I'm really proud of some of
the things I've achieved.

00:17:17.718 --> 00:17:21.885
But I wish I had not put
myself so low in the totem pole

00:17:22.809 --> 00:17:24.945
when I was 18 or 19 years old,

00:17:24.945 --> 00:17:27.219
and thought that these other people

00:17:27.219 --> 00:17:29.030
who were sitting up on stage talking,

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:30.706
knew so much more than me,

00:17:30.706 --> 00:17:33.954
and had happy, problem-free lives,

00:17:33.954 --> 00:17:35.954
because that's not true.

00:17:37.076 --> 00:17:38.809
I'm not saying that to depress you.

00:17:38.809 --> 00:17:42.118
I'm actually trying to say that as a way

00:17:42.118 --> 00:17:45.618
to sort of liberate yourself a little bit.

00:17:46.506 --> 00:17:50.849
It's not allow for mistakes,
allow for problems.

00:17:50.849 --> 00:17:55.194
I've had my career blow up on
me about two different times.

00:17:55.194 --> 00:17:57.671
And I'm still here, and very grateful.

00:17:57.671 --> 00:18:00.540
And I learned a lot during those moments,

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:03.903
and wouldn't really change
a thing about any of it.

00:18:03.903 --> 00:18:07.403
And so I think Harvard, as great as it is,

00:18:08.779 --> 00:18:10.362
can sometimes instill,

00:18:10.362 --> 00:18:12.375
and I don't think it's,

00:18:12.375 --> 00:18:13.595
no one's telling you this,

00:18:13.595 --> 00:18:15.869
it's just subliminal.

00:18:15.869 --> 00:18:18.323
A lot of you have this is,
"I have to do great things,

00:18:18.323 --> 00:18:20.395
"and I need to succeed."

00:18:20.395 --> 00:18:22.787
And you just have to
be willing to screw up

00:18:22.787 --> 00:18:25.499
and not freak out when you do screw up.

00:18:25.499 --> 00:18:26.750
'Cause you will screw up.

00:18:26.750 --> 00:18:30.195
I mean, you're really going to screw up.

00:18:30.195 --> 00:18:31.445
It's in a week.

00:18:32.847 --> 00:18:34.264
We'll talk later.

00:18:35.669 --> 00:18:37.769
What I'm doing is I've
made a conscious decision,

00:18:37.769 --> 00:18:39.473
I'm not going to try and do what they do.

00:18:39.473 --> 00:18:42.175
I didn't spend this summer
going to comedy clubs.

00:18:42.175 --> 00:18:44.305
I'm a real person, I'm
just going to go out there,

00:18:44.305 --> 00:18:47.711
and say things that I think are funny,

00:18:47.711 --> 00:18:49.373
and just be Conan.

00:18:49.373 --> 00:18:50.985
And I think that so far,

00:18:50.985 --> 00:18:53.352
I go out there and I'll tell a few jokes

00:18:53.352 --> 00:18:54.828
or stories every night,

00:18:54.828 --> 00:18:56.443
and the audience laughs,

00:18:56.443 --> 00:18:58.106
and they're things that I think are funny,

00:18:58.106 --> 00:19:01.624
and I think I'm presenting
them in a way that's honest,

00:19:01.624 --> 00:19:04.000
in a way that it's just Conan.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:05.706
It's just Conan talking.

00:19:05.706 --> 00:19:07.535
And that's all I've ever
wanted from this show.

00:19:07.535 --> 00:19:09.832
I just want this show to be,

00:19:09.832 --> 00:19:13.590
my only goal is that I
get myself out there 100%.

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:16.141
If people don't like that,
and don't accept that,

00:19:16.141 --> 00:19:17.818
I'll say, "Well, that's all I can do."

00:19:17.818 --> 00:19:19.248
- But sometimes it's even hard to do that.

00:19:19.248 --> 00:19:20.621
I mean, it's hard to make sure

00:19:20.621 --> 00:19:21.979
that you are natural and comfortable,

00:19:21.979 --> 00:19:23.333
and that what you are--

00:19:23.333 --> 00:19:24.387
- That's what takes time.

00:19:24.387 --> 00:19:27.145
I mean, it took Dave a while.

00:19:27.145 --> 00:19:29.398
It's going to take me a while too.

00:19:29.398 --> 00:19:32.311
It's a very strange thing to say,

00:19:32.311 --> 00:19:34.484
"What we'll like you to do is go out there

00:19:34.484 --> 00:19:37.868
"and just be yourself,
completely uninhibited,

00:19:37.868 --> 00:19:40.004
"on a set in front of cameras,

00:19:40.004 --> 00:19:42.412
"200 people in the audience,

00:19:42.412 --> 00:19:44.880
"and we'll critique you every night.

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:46.635
"And critiques will watch

00:19:46.635 --> 00:19:48.494
"for when you rub your finger together,

00:19:48.494 --> 00:19:52.701
"or touch your nose,
or anything like that."

00:19:52.701 --> 00:19:53.858
It takes a little while

00:19:53.858 --> 00:19:58.154
to become completely
unselfconscious in that environment.

00:19:58.154 --> 00:20:02.391
- How is it different today
in doing your show late night

00:20:02.391 --> 00:20:04.385
than it was 10 years ago?

00:20:04.385 --> 00:20:06.468
Are you more instinctive?

00:20:08.729 --> 00:20:10.626
Do you think less about
it, and just do it?

00:20:10.626 --> 00:20:11.734
- Yeah, it's muscle memory.

00:20:11.734 --> 00:20:13.059
- [Charlie] It is.

00:20:13.059 --> 00:20:13.916
Like the golf swing.

00:20:13.916 --> 00:20:15.168
- You crunch down.

00:20:15.168 --> 00:20:18.591
And you're constantly trying to learn.

00:20:18.591 --> 00:20:20.045
You're still thinking,

00:20:20.045 --> 00:20:23.807
but your ratio of thinking
to acting is very different.

00:20:23.807 --> 00:20:28.303
At the beginning, I was
thinking about it this much

00:20:28.303 --> 00:20:29.732
in my cognitive brain,

00:20:29.732 --> 00:20:31.269
and then there was this much,

00:20:31.269 --> 00:20:34.809
just whatever thin reed of
talent you have is there.

00:20:34.809 --> 00:20:36.424
And then you're thinking about the rest.

00:20:36.424 --> 00:20:37.998
And then over the years I think,

00:20:37.998 --> 00:20:41.709
they always say the reptile
portion of your brain

00:20:41.709 --> 00:20:44.809
is where respiration, heart beat.

00:20:44.809 --> 00:20:47.029
That's the stuff that even if
you're knocked on conscious,

00:20:47.029 --> 00:20:48.322
it still working.

00:20:48.322 --> 00:20:51.063
I really believe a lot of my
talk show or comedy instincts

00:20:51.063 --> 00:20:53.103
are now in my reptile brain.

00:20:53.103 --> 00:20:57.265
That you could knock me out,
and my heart would still beat,

00:20:57.265 --> 00:20:58.593
I'd still breathe,

00:20:58.593 --> 00:21:01.958
and I'll still be asking Lindsay
Lohen some stupid question.

00:21:01.958 --> 00:21:03.360
- You have become,

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:05.054
some way as part of your DNA,

00:21:05.054 --> 00:21:06.463
it's become part of your DNA.

00:21:06.463 --> 00:21:08.151
- Yeah, I think a lot of it is,

00:21:08.151 --> 00:21:10.119
you have to have some ability.

00:21:10.119 --> 00:21:11.625
But then it's just how much will.

00:21:11.625 --> 00:21:13.699
I really do think there's just,

00:21:13.699 --> 00:21:14.913
I think people underestimate.

00:21:14.913 --> 00:21:16.497
It doesn't sound sexy.

00:21:16.497 --> 00:21:17.917
It doesn't sound cool.

00:21:17.917 --> 00:21:18.965
- How much do you want it?

00:21:18.965 --> 00:21:20.770
- Work ethic.

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:23.440
And just how badly do you want it?

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:25.940
And there have been many times

00:21:27.589 --> 00:21:29.150
throughout the years of doing my show,

00:21:29.150 --> 00:21:31.318
where I've thought, "You
can shoot me in the chest

00:21:31.318 --> 00:21:32.598
"before I walk out there,

00:21:32.598 --> 00:21:34.609
"and I'll still walk out
there and do that show.

00:21:34.609 --> 00:21:36.663
"And then I'll come backstage and die.

00:21:36.663 --> 00:21:38.510
"But we're doing that show."

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:42.591
- It's been a lifelong dream
of mine to touch your hair.

00:21:42.591 --> 00:21:45.591
(audience cheering)

00:22:20.922 --> 00:22:24.239
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
about you working here.

00:22:24.239 --> 00:22:26.489
- I've solved that problem.

00:22:27.848 --> 00:22:31.356
- Guys, no more touching
questions, please.

00:22:31.356 --> 00:22:32.793
- What are you talking about?

00:22:32.793 --> 00:22:33.964
Yes.

00:22:33.964 --> 00:22:35.464
Let's touch it up.

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:37.923
I'm sorry.

00:22:37.923 --> 00:22:39.063
I feel really crass.

00:22:39.063 --> 00:22:42.134
I don't even know your name.

00:22:42.134 --> 00:22:44.440
Can I just know your name?

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:46.575
'Cause later on my wife's going
to be like, "What happened?"

00:22:46.575 --> 00:22:50.364
"I don't know, I don't
even know her name."

00:22:50.364 --> 00:22:51.268
"What did you do?"

00:22:51.268 --> 00:22:54.329
"We rubbed out heads together."

00:22:54.329 --> 00:22:55.718
What is your name?

00:22:55.718 --> 00:22:56.754
- My name's Kelly.

00:22:56.754 --> 00:22:58.552
- Hi, Kelly.

00:22:58.552 --> 00:23:00.107
We should've done this before we rubbed up

00:23:00.107 --> 00:23:02.439
against each other.

00:23:02.439 --> 00:23:03.427
Hey there internet.

00:23:03.427 --> 00:23:04.749
Conan O'Brien here.

00:23:04.749 --> 00:23:06.717
Now, a lot of people
have been calling me out

00:23:06.717 --> 00:23:10.204
on this ALS Ice Bucket Challenge.

00:23:10.204 --> 00:23:12.457
I just want to say I accept.

00:23:12.457 --> 00:23:15.030
This sounds like a really sweet deal.

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:18.548
Apparently if I get ice
water poured on my head,

00:23:18.548 --> 00:23:20.715
ALS will then pay me $100.

00:23:22.884 --> 00:23:24.973
That's a pretty sweet deal.

00:23:24.973 --> 00:23:26.358
I could use that cash.

00:23:26.358 --> 00:23:27.625
Let's do it.

00:23:27.625 --> 00:23:28.458
All right.

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:33.475
Wow, that was cold.

00:23:33.475 --> 00:23:36.975
Okay, ALS send the check to Conan O'Brien,

00:23:37.975 --> 00:23:40.078
care of the accountancy firm--

00:23:40.078 --> 00:23:42.144
- [Man] Hello, you don't get paid.

00:23:42.144 --> 00:23:44.061
You get dunked and pay.

00:23:44.990 --> 00:23:45.823
- I pay?

00:23:45.823 --> 00:23:47.093
- [Man] Yes, it's a charity.

00:23:47.093 --> 00:23:48.146
- Why would I do both?

00:23:48.146 --> 00:23:50.216
- [Man] Why would anybody
be giving you $100?

00:23:50.216 --> 00:23:52.992
- 'Cause I got a lot of ice
cold water poured on me.

00:23:52.992 --> 00:23:54.089
- [Man] But it's a charity,

00:23:54.089 --> 00:23:54.966
they're not going to give you money.

00:23:54.966 --> 00:23:56.216
- So I don't get paid anything?

00:23:56.216 --> 00:23:57.749
- Do you need $100?
- That's ridiculous.

00:23:57.749 --> 00:23:58.849
- [Man] Do you really need $100?

00:23:58.849 --> 00:23:59.682
- That's stupid.

00:23:59.682 --> 00:24:00.655
Hey, well guess what?

00:24:00.655 --> 00:24:02.317
Psych, ALS.

00:24:02.317 --> 00:24:03.631
Check this out.

00:24:03.631 --> 00:24:04.631
Plastic ice.

00:24:06.556 --> 00:24:08.852
That water was a balmy 92 degrees.

00:24:08.852 --> 00:24:10.102
I felt nothing.

00:24:12.832 --> 00:24:14.332
Oh, that was real!

00:24:15.285 --> 00:24:18.118
(Conan screaming)

00:24:21.886 --> 00:24:23.850
- [Man] Okay, you got
to challenge people now.

00:24:23.850 --> 00:24:24.683
- Oh yeah.

00:24:24.683 --> 00:24:28.850
I challenge Jimmy Carter,
Paley, Harry Joel Osment.

00:24:31.648 --> 00:24:34.481
(audience cheers)

00:24:36.352 --> 00:24:37.866
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:24:37.866 --> 00:24:40.684
I made this video because
David Mares asked me to.

00:24:40.684 --> 00:24:41.988
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:24:41.988 --> 00:24:43.451
you want me to profile next,

00:24:43.451 --> 00:24:44.836
leave it down in the comments below,

00:24:44.836 --> 00:24:45.950
and I'll see what I can do.

00:24:45.950 --> 00:24:47.718
I also love to know what did Conan say

00:24:47.718 --> 00:24:49.803
that really inspired
you, or motivated you,

00:24:49.803 --> 00:24:51.283
or had an impact on you.

00:24:51.283 --> 00:24:53.930
What was the most important
message you got from this video?

00:24:53.930 --> 00:24:55.512
Leave it down on the comments below.

00:24:55.512 --> 00:24:56.962
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:24:56.962 --> 00:24:58.713
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:24:58.713 --> 00:25:00.010
I believe in you.

00:25:00.010 --> 00:25:01.802
I hope you believe in yourself.

00:25:01.802 --> 00:25:04.096
And whatever your one word is,

00:25:04.096 --> 00:25:05.596
I'll see you soon.

00:25:11.252 --> 00:25:13.068
- Well, lowest moment,

00:25:13.068 --> 00:25:15.153
you were actually part of.

00:25:15.153 --> 00:25:19.070
I was on the air for
about two months, I think,

00:25:21.058 --> 00:25:23.712
and Chevy Chase had just been canceled.

00:25:23.712 --> 00:25:24.874
And my staff was happy.

00:25:24.874 --> 00:25:27.081
They said, "Oh, at least
we've outlasted Chevy Chase."

00:25:27.081 --> 00:25:29.476
And I remembered saying,
"You don't understand.

00:25:29.476 --> 00:25:31.548
"The critics just destroyed one show.

00:25:31.548 --> 00:25:32.662
"There's blood in the water now,

00:25:32.662 --> 00:25:34.927
"they're going to look
who's next in line."

00:25:34.927 --> 00:25:37.550
They didn't have to look very far.

00:25:37.550 --> 00:25:41.505
David Letterman replaced by
this guy from total obscurity,

00:25:41.505 --> 00:25:43.195
and the show had a lot of flaws.

00:25:43.195 --> 00:25:44.308
They weren't wrong.

00:25:44.308 --> 00:25:48.475
I was new, and was making
rookie mistakes on the air.

00:25:49.547 --> 00:25:51.718
So they came looking for me.

00:25:51.718 --> 00:25:53.303
And I was on this show,

00:25:53.303 --> 00:25:55.515
I'm thinking it's maybe October, November.

00:25:55.515 --> 00:25:56.348
- Yeah, exactly.

00:25:56.348 --> 00:25:57.281
You haven't been on long.

00:25:57.281 --> 00:25:58.114
- Of 1993.

00:25:58.114 --> 00:25:58.947
I haven't been on long.

00:25:58.947 --> 00:26:01.115
You had me on the program.

00:26:01.115 --> 00:26:02.633
And we shot it in the morning.

00:26:02.633 --> 00:26:03.995
And you started the interview by saying,

00:26:03.995 --> 00:26:05.073
"Conan, I don't know if you've seen

00:26:05.073 --> 00:26:06.594
"the Washington Post this morning,

00:26:06.594 --> 00:26:08.700
"but Tom Shales reviewed your show."

00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:11.754
And then you started to read this review.

00:26:11.754 --> 00:26:13.250
- [Charlie] What a cruel thing to do.

00:26:13.250 --> 00:26:15.462
- Well, I respected you
for doing it at the time.

00:26:15.462 --> 00:26:17.169
That's how I get most of my information,

00:26:17.169 --> 00:26:19.960
is being told by other talk show hosts.

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:22.043
And so I read the review,

00:26:23.142 --> 00:26:26.573
and then went back to my office.

00:26:26.573 --> 00:26:29.647
And climbed underneath
the desk in my office.

00:26:29.647 --> 00:26:31.430
And laid down for a while.

00:26:31.430 --> 00:26:32.370
- [Charlie] Put a blanket over your head.

00:26:32.370 --> 00:26:33.203
- No, I didn't.

00:26:33.203 --> 00:26:34.036
I just laid down.

00:26:34.036 --> 00:26:35.104
And I have very long legs,

00:26:35.104 --> 00:26:36.902
and they stuck up from
underneath the desk.

00:26:36.902 --> 00:26:38.046
And the word got around the office

00:26:38.046 --> 00:26:41.298
that Conan is on the
floor underneath his desk.

00:26:41.298 --> 00:26:43.444
And I remembered there
being a lot of murmuring.

00:26:43.444 --> 00:26:45.168
And the producer of our show, Jeff Ross,

00:26:45.168 --> 00:26:46.997
leaning in and said, "You okay?"

00:26:46.997 --> 00:26:48.717
And I went, "I'm okay.

00:26:48.717 --> 00:26:51.168
"I'm just under my desk,
but then I'll get out."

00:26:51.168 --> 00:26:53.516
And then from there,

00:26:53.516 --> 00:26:57.202
I really do believe that
I wanted to do this show

00:26:57.202 --> 00:27:00.619
more than anybody else didn't want me to.

00:27:01.734 --> 00:27:03.466
And I knew we had the elements.

00:27:03.466 --> 00:27:05.779
I knew that we had all the
elements of a good show.

00:27:05.779 --> 00:27:07.306
I had a very creative,

00:27:07.306 --> 00:27:08.625
and still have a very creative

00:27:08.625 --> 00:27:10.571
and adventurous writing staff.

00:27:10.571 --> 00:27:12.715
Andy Richter's a very funny guy,

00:27:12.715 --> 00:27:15.385
and there's not a presence
like him on television.

00:27:15.385 --> 00:27:17.624
The Max Weinberg 7 is an amazing band

00:27:17.624 --> 00:27:19.788
that can play swing music,

00:27:19.788 --> 00:27:22.000
but can also play rockabilly,

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:23.293
they can play funk.

00:27:23.293 --> 00:27:25.696
They can do anything.

00:27:25.696 --> 00:27:27.149
And I knew that we have all the elements.

00:27:27.149 --> 00:27:29.101
And I knew that I had the elements,

00:27:29.101 --> 00:27:31.217
they just weren't all there yet.

00:27:31.217 --> 00:27:33.822
It's like a baby skull
has to come together.

00:27:33.822 --> 00:27:35.118
Isn't that a nice vision?

00:27:35.118 --> 00:27:36.032
- Just a baby skull.

00:27:36.032 --> 00:27:37.523
- Yeah, I really like a baby skull.

00:27:37.523 --> 00:27:38.570
- I think I told you the time,

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:39.424
as you reminded me,

00:27:39.424 --> 00:27:40.886
just to stay on the field.

00:27:40.886 --> 00:27:41.950
- That's what you told me.

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:43.594
You said that, "Remember
what Joe DiMaggio said,

00:27:43.594 --> 00:27:44.442
"'Stay on the field.'"

00:27:44.442 --> 00:27:46.848
And at the time, I walked
out of here and said,

00:27:46.848 --> 00:27:47.681
"What an ass.

00:27:47.681 --> 00:27:48.514
"What an idiot.

00:27:48.514 --> 00:27:49.729
"What's he talking about?

00:27:49.729 --> 00:27:50.590
"Stay on the field."

00:27:50.590 --> 00:27:51.914
- That's me or Joe DiMaggio?

00:27:51.914 --> 00:27:52.747
- Both of you.

00:27:52.747 --> 00:27:53.776
I love you both together.

00:27:53.776 --> 00:27:57.939
I have contempt for anyone
who's achieved anything.

00:27:57.939 --> 00:27:59.161
But it's good advice,

00:27:59.161 --> 00:28:02.365
which is keeping doing your show.

00:28:02.365 --> 00:28:05.628
And the network put
through my paces a bit.

00:28:05.628 --> 00:28:07.373
And they put me through somethings.

00:28:07.373 --> 00:28:09.748
- [Charlie] One week extensions.

00:28:09.748 --> 00:28:12.394
- Yeah, I was being renewed
hourly at one point.

00:28:12.394 --> 00:28:14.521
- "You can do this show, but no more.

00:28:14.521 --> 00:28:15.692
"We're not making any promises

00:28:15.692 --> 00:28:16.817
"after this show."
- "We'll let you know

00:28:16.817 --> 00:28:18.431
"in 40 minutes, if you're
still a talk show host."

00:28:18.431 --> 00:28:19.931
- [Charlie] "We may
jerk you doing this show

00:28:19.931 --> 00:28:20.939
"if it doesn't go well."

00:28:20.939 --> 00:28:22.704
- But I remembered a lot of
people saying at the time,

00:28:22.704 --> 00:28:24.506
"Well, I wouldn't stand for that.

00:28:24.506 --> 00:28:25.510
"I'd walk out."

00:28:25.510 --> 00:28:27.807
And I remembered thinking,
"No, I'm going to stay here

00:28:27.807 --> 00:28:29.320
"and do my show,

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:31.557
"and people are eventually going to see

00:28:31.557 --> 00:28:33.415
"that we've got something here.

00:28:33.415 --> 00:28:35.091
"And I'm going to get better at this."

00:28:35.091 --> 00:28:39.060
I do think I have ticks and mannerisms.

00:28:39.060 --> 00:28:41.578
And I think sometimes
there's a tendency these days

00:28:41.578 --> 00:28:43.465
to think we've got to
get rid of all of those,

00:28:43.465 --> 00:28:47.553
and be just a completely smooth performer.

00:28:47.553 --> 00:28:49.064
And what I think is nice.

00:28:49.064 --> 00:28:51.434
No, my voice isn't perfect for TV.

00:28:51.434 --> 00:28:54.184
I mean, can't you tell?

00:28:54.184 --> 00:28:57.960
There's things about me
that aren't perfect for TV.

00:28:57.960 --> 00:28:59.436
There are times when you can see

00:28:59.436 --> 00:29:02.494
that I'm just a little throne.

00:29:02.494 --> 00:29:05.494
Or you can see that I touch my nose,

00:29:06.370 --> 00:29:07.507
or I do something like that.

00:29:07.507 --> 00:29:09.346
And sometimes I think,

00:29:09.346 --> 00:29:11.297
"Don't get rid of all that."

00:29:11.297 --> 00:29:13.966
In a way, I think I'm
just a real person, and--

00:29:13.966 --> 00:29:15.398
- Because it makes you authentic.

00:29:15.398 --> 00:29:17.565
- Yeah, I think it's real.

00:29:18.665 --> 00:29:22.149
Again, I say if for any
reason down the road,

00:29:22.149 --> 00:29:25.091
NBC would decide, "Well,
this show didn't work."

00:29:25.091 --> 00:29:26.322
I'm proud.

00:29:26.322 --> 00:29:28.640
I think the humor reflects my sensibility.

00:29:28.640 --> 00:29:31.566
I think we're going for
the music and the guests

00:29:31.566 --> 00:29:34.540
that reflect my sensibility,
for the most part.

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:36.673
And we're doing the show I wanted to do.

00:29:36.673 --> 00:29:39.535
And so I would not feel bitterness or sad.

00:29:39.535 --> 00:29:42.505
I would be sad because I enjoy
doing it, if it were to end.

00:29:42.505 --> 00:29:45.204
But I think we're doing what I want to do.

00:29:45.204 --> 00:29:48.578
There are few things more
liberating in this life

00:29:48.578 --> 00:29:51.625
than having your worst fear realized.

00:29:51.625 --> 00:29:53.423
I went to college with many people

00:29:53.423 --> 00:29:56.319
who prided themselves on
knowing exactly who they were

00:29:56.319 --> 00:29:58.774
and exactly where they were going.

00:29:58.774 --> 00:30:01.103
At Harvard, five
different guys in my class

00:30:01.103 --> 00:30:02.719
told me they would one day be president

00:30:02.719 --> 00:30:04.351
of the Unites States.

00:30:04.351 --> 00:30:07.472
Four of them were later
killed in motel shootouts.

00:30:07.472 --> 00:30:09.269
The other ones briefly hosted Blues Clues

00:30:09.269 --> 00:30:12.458
before senselessly in yet
another motel shootout.

00:30:12.458 --> 00:30:16.315
Your path at 22 will not
necessarily be your path

00:30:16.315 --> 00:30:17.315
at 32 or 42.

00:30:18.936 --> 00:30:21.022
One's dream is constantly evolving,

00:30:21.022 --> 00:30:22.496
rising and falling,

00:30:22.496 --> 00:30:23.975
changing course.

00:30:23.975 --> 00:30:25.119
This happens in every job.

00:30:25.119 --> 00:30:27.544
But because I have worked
in comedy for 25 years,

00:30:27.544 --> 00:30:31.598
I can probably speak best
about my own profession.

00:30:31.598 --> 00:30:33.873
Way back in the 1940s,

00:30:33.873 --> 00:30:37.970
there was a very, very
funny man named Jack Benny.

00:30:37.970 --> 00:30:40.682
He was a giant star, easily
one of the greatest comedians

00:30:40.682 --> 00:30:42.297
of his generation.

00:30:42.297 --> 00:30:44.979
And a much younger man
named Johnny Carson,

00:30:44.979 --> 00:30:47.936
who wanted very much to be Jack Benny.

00:30:47.936 --> 00:30:49.747
In some ways he was.

00:30:49.747 --> 00:30:52.503
But in many ways, he wasn't.

00:30:52.503 --> 00:30:53.752
He emulated Jack Benny,

00:30:53.752 --> 00:30:55.598
but his own quirks and mannerisms,

00:30:55.598 --> 00:30:57.253
along with the changing medium,

00:30:57.253 --> 00:30:59.421
pulled him in a different direction.

00:30:59.421 --> 00:31:03.000
And yet his failure to
completely become his hero

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:06.703
made him the funniest
person of his generation.

00:31:06.703 --> 00:31:09.326
David Letterman wanted
to be Johnny Carson,

00:31:09.326 --> 00:31:10.453
and was not.

00:31:10.453 --> 00:31:11.476
And as a result,

00:31:11.476 --> 00:31:15.512
my generation of comedians
wanted to be David Letterman.

00:31:15.512 --> 00:31:17.370
And none of us are.

00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:19.156
My peers and I have all missed that mark

00:31:19.156 --> 00:31:21.059
in a thousand different ways.

00:31:21.059 --> 00:31:22.661
But the point is this,

00:31:22.661 --> 00:31:26.562
it is our failure to
become our perceived ideal

00:31:26.562 --> 00:31:30.110
that ultimately defines
us and makes us unique.

00:31:30.110 --> 00:31:31.712
It's not easy.

00:31:31.712 --> 00:31:33.706
But if you accept your misfortune,

00:31:33.706 --> 00:31:35.261
and handle it right,

00:31:35.261 --> 00:31:38.187
your perceived failure
can become a catalyst

00:31:38.187 --> 00:31:40.270
for profound reinvention.

00:31:51.283 --> 00:31:52.299
So at the age of 47,

00:31:52.299 --> 00:31:56.169
after 25 years of obsessively
pursuing my dream,

00:31:56.169 --> 00:31:57.619
that dream changed.

00:31:57.619 --> 00:31:59.066
For decades in show business,

00:31:59.066 --> 00:32:00.484
the ultimate goal of every comedian

00:32:00.484 --> 00:32:02.525
was to host The Tonight Show.

00:32:02.525 --> 00:32:04.189
It was the holy grail.

00:32:04.189 --> 00:32:06.396
And like many people, I thought
that achieving that goal

00:32:06.396 --> 00:32:09.078
would define me as successful.

00:32:09.078 --> 00:32:10.892
But that is not true.

00:32:10.892 --> 00:32:13.768
No specific job or career goal defines me,

00:32:13.768 --> 00:32:16.185
and it should not define you.

00:32:20.093 --> 00:32:23.505
In 2000, I told graduates
to not be afraid to fail,

00:32:23.505 --> 00:32:25.516
and I still believe that.

00:32:25.516 --> 00:32:28.337
But today I tell you that
whether you fear it or not,

00:32:28.337 --> 00:32:30.440
disappointment will come.

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:32.510
The beauty is that through disappointment

00:32:32.510 --> 00:32:34.128
you can gain clarity,

00:32:34.128 --> 00:32:38.834
and with clarity comes
conviction and true originality.

00:32:38.834 --> 00:32:40.754
Many of you here today
are getting your diploma

00:32:40.754 --> 00:32:41.674
at this Ivy League school

00:32:41.674 --> 00:32:43.475
because you have committed
yourself to a dream,

00:32:43.475 --> 00:32:45.302
and worked hard to achieve it.

00:32:45.302 --> 00:32:47.436
And there is no greater cliche
in a commencement address

00:32:47.436 --> 00:32:49.460
than follow your dream.

00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:51.576
Well, I'm here to tell you,

00:32:51.576 --> 00:32:54.062
that whatever you think your dream is now,

00:32:54.062 --> 00:32:56.042
it will probably change.

00:32:56.042 --> 00:32:57.962
And that's okay.

00:32:57.962 --> 00:33:00.962
Four years ago, many of
you had specific vision

00:33:00.962 --> 00:33:03.196
of what your college
experience was going to be,

00:33:03.196 --> 00:33:05.155
and who you were going to become.

00:33:05.155 --> 00:33:06.831
And I bet today, most of you would admit

00:33:06.831 --> 00:33:08.310
that your time here was very different

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:09.857
from what you imagined.

00:33:09.857 --> 00:33:12.163
Your roommates changed,
your major changed,

00:33:12.163 --> 00:33:15.178
for some of you your
sexual orientation changed.

00:33:15.178 --> 00:33:17.326
I bet some of you have changed
your sexual orientation

00:33:17.326 --> 00:33:20.160
since I began this speech.

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:21.327
I know I have.

00:33:23.756 --> 00:33:26.529
But through the good,
and especially the bad,

00:33:26.529 --> 00:33:27.657
the person that you are now

00:33:27.657 --> 00:33:29.424
is someone you could never have conjured

00:33:29.424 --> 00:33:30.937
in the Fall of 2007.

